{ignore}
<p><code>{echo}</code> evaluates the expression written after its name and prints the value.
The whole text up to the closing brace is one expression - it is not split into parameters -
so <code>{echo $price * $qty}</code>, <code>{echo $n gt 1 ? 'items' : 'item'}</code> and
<code>{echo 'Hello ' . $name}</code> all work. A quoted text is a literal: <code>{echo 5 + 3}</code>
prints 8, <code>{echo '5 + 3'}</code> prints the text itself.</p>

<p>It is the tag to put in front of a pipe when the value is a literal or an expression:
<code>{echo $text | trim | upper}</code>, <code>{echo $value | + 1}</code> (an arithmetic pipe
needs the space after its operator). A field can be piped directly as well -
<code>{$name | upper}</code> - and that is the difference that matters: the field tag runs
the sanitize chain, <code>{echo}</code> does not. <code>{echo $html}</code> writes the value as
it stands, markup and all, so use <code>{$html}</code> for anything a visitor typed and keep
<code>{echo}</code> for values the application made itself.</p>

<p>A value is text, never template code: with <code>$v = '{php:getcwd}'</code>,
<code>{echo $v}</code> prints those characters rather than running them. TRUE prints as
<code>1</code>, FALSE and NULL as nothing.</p>

<p>Under the strict syntax check an <code>{echo}</code> with nothing before its first pipe -
or nothing at all - is an error, as is an undefined field or a pipe function that does not
exist.</p>
{/ignore}
